The callback in `setInterval` is a normal function, not an arrow function. Normal functions get their own `this`. When `setInterval` calls the function, `this` does not point to the `Timer` instance.

As a result, `this.seconds` is `undefined`. The line `this.seconds += 1` computes `undefined + 1`, which is `NaN`. Thus each tick prints `NaN`.

Fix: use an arrow function for the callback. Arrow functions do not have their own `this`. They use `this` from the surrounding scope, which is the `Timer` instance.

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
  }
  start() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds += 1;
      console.log(this.seconds);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
new Timer().start();
```

Alternative fix: bind the function to `this`.

```javascript
setInterval(function () {
  this.seconds += 1;
  console.log(this.seconds);
}.bind(this), 1000);
```

The arrow function is the simpler fix.
